## Cognitive Cache (MCP)

`select_context_tool` is an opt-in cold-start orientation aid, not an enforced step and not a context oracle. Benchmarking is honest about its ceiling: it beats naive grep and TF-IDF on retrieval (SWE-bench recall@5 ~0.54 vs grep ~0.36), but it does NOT beat a capable agent doing its own grep/read on tasks where the files are findable; on single-file localization a plain grep+read loop matches or beats it. It returns full file *contents* up to the token budget, so a call front-loads context (a real token cost) rather than saving it. Treat it as "orient faster in a repo I don't know," never as "always run this."

**CALL it only when ALL of these hold:**
- You are genuinely cold in a LARGE, unfamiliar repo (you have not worked here this session and cannot name the files).
- The task spans multiple files you cannot name, and locating them by hand would take many grep/read roundtrips.
- The orientation is worth pulling up to ~12k tokens of file bodies into context.

**DO NOT call it when any of:**
- You can name the file you would grep for. Read it directly.
- The repo is small/medium or one you can navigate; your own grep is better and cheaper.
- You have already read the relevant files this session, or you know where to look.
- It is a single-file edit at a known path, or a mechanical change (rename, type fix, syntax).
- You just want to "double-check" — the token cost is not worth a confirmation.

**How to phrase the `task` argument (if you do call it):**
- Mention symbol names verbatim; symbol overlap is the dominant signal (weight ~0.45).
- Mention file basenames if you have hunches; path-stem matching is wired in.
- Precise beats vague: "users get 401 after OIDC callback when session token is present" beats "fix auth bug".
- Multiple targeted queries beat one vague query; the index is cached per repo path.

**How to read the response:**
- It includes file *contents*; use them directly, do not re-Read a file it returned.
- Check `used_fallback`. If true, nothing cleared the confidence floor; treat the ranking as best-effort and verify with grep.
- If `signals.symbol_overlap` is 0.0 across all returned files, the tool guessed; do not trust the order.
- It misses the right files often; it is a starting hint to verify, not an authority.
